  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\n   Central idea:\n\n   We aim to find a step function on a grid of points (initially using grids of 70, 100, and 140) that minimizes the Erds minimum overlap constant C_5. We start from the provided 70-point solution and use interpolation to create an initial candidate for other grid resolutions. We then apply an optimization algorithm (scipy.optimize.minimize) with bounds and constraints to find a better candidate. The optimization uses finite-difference gradients and respects the constraints that the function takes values in [0,1] and has total integral exactly (n_points/2) for the grid-size-specific normalization.\n\n   This approach is non-convex and relies on local search. We hope to escape local optima by starting from different interpolations. We allocate time budget to multiple grid sizes.\n\n   Addressed by bottlenecks:\n\n     - The objective is non-convex.\n     - The constraints and bounds are complex.\n     - The function evaluation is expensive (full convolution).\n\n   We accept that this might not be global optimal but hope for a significant improvement within the time budget.\n\n   Limitations:\n\n     - The optimizationalgorithm may get stuck in local optima. To mitigate, we use multiple restarts (interpolations from the parent) and different grid sizes.\n     - The finite-difference gradient estimate is noisy and expensive.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n   Variables:\n\n     - h: a vector of length n in (\u211d)^n \n     - normalized to [0,1] elements\n\n   Objective function:\n\n     - We minimize C_5 = dx * max_{k} (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')[k]), where dx=2.0/n.\n\n   Constraints:\n\n     - Each h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i.\n     - sum(h) = n/2.\n\n   Representation:\n\n     - Grid solution using sample points from 0 to 2 at spacing 2/n_points.\n\n   Optimization formulation:\n\n     - We minimize C_5 using scipy.optimize.minimize with the SLSQP or trust-constr method, which handles both bound constraints and equality constraints.\n\n     - The function has an equality constraint: sum(h) = n/2.\n\n   Variables, indices, dimensions:\n\n     - h is a 1D array of length n.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n   Preprocessing:\n\n     - Given the initial_h_values (parent solution on 70-point grid)\n     - For a new grid size n, interpolate the parent to get an initial guess.\n\n   Function definitions:\n\n     - interpolate_parent(parent, n): uses linear interpolation to create a new array of length n.\n     - objective(h, n): computes C_5 using correlation.\n\n   Optimization:\n\n     - Use scipy.optimize.minimize with method='SLSQP' or 'trust-constr' and budget_s seconds time limit.\n\n     - Define bounds for each element of h being in [0,1].\n     - Define constraint as sum(h) - n/2 = 0.\n\n   Budget allocation:\n\n     - Allocate ~300 seconds for the 70-point grid (parent interpolation)\n     - Allocate ~500 seconds for the 100-point grid\n     - Allocate ~500 seconds for the 140-point grid\n\n   Checks and repair:\n\n     - After optimization, verify that resulting h satisfies 0<=h[i]<=1.\n     - Adjust the solution to meet the integral constraint (n/2).\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n   - Interpolation frequency: interpolating from the parent solution for higher grids.\n   - Optimization method: SLSQP or trust-constr for better constraint handling.\n   - Gradient computation: Finite-difference approximated.\n   - Budget distribution: Prioritize larger grids for potential improvement.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n   Not detailed here.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n   Check that the returned h satisfies 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h) = n/2. Use scipy.optimize.minimize with appropriate settings for bounded constraint handling.\n\n   Return the best solution found (h_values), the corresponding C_5 (best found), and n_points.\n\n   The method will be applicable to any grid size, with interpolation from the parent if n!=70.",
